Patch 80 -- the present-latency wait becomes a semaphore, and the hand-written
wake protocol it used to guard is deleted.

SCOPE: dlls/wined3d/{cs.c,swapchain.c,wined3d_private.h}. This is a backport of
Wine 11.15's mechanism, not a local design; the differences from upstream are
listed below and are deliberate.

WHAT P81A DID, AND WHY IT WAS A NET

FINALPLAY13 turned the producer's INFINITE wait on cs->present_event into a
250 ms recheck of pending_presents, because the 2026-08-23 freeze showed the wait
outliving its own condition. The reasoning was right: the event was only the
wakeup mechanism and never the condition, so one lost SetEvent() wedges the game
permanently even after pending_presents has dropped. But the result is a timer
stretched over a protocol -- three pieces of shared state (pending_presents,
waiting_for_present, present_event) that have to stay in step, plus an injection
switch (MGS2_PRESENT_DROP_WAKE) whose only purpose was to prove the net catches a
protocol bug that should not be possible.

WHAT UPSTREAM DOES INSTEAD

Wine 11.15 replaced all of it with one counting semaphore per swapchain:

  wined3d_swapchain_present()    WaitForSingleObject(frame_latency_semaphore)
                                 before taking the D3D mutex
  wined3d_cs_exec_present()      ReleaseSemaphore(frame_latency_semaphore, 1)
                                 after the present has actually been executed

The count IS the condition. There is no second flag to fall out of step with it,
and a release that lands before the wait is not lost -- it was already counted. So
pending_presents, waiting_for_present, present_event, the recheck timeout,
MGS2_PRESENT_RECHECK_MS and MGS2_PRESENT_DROP_WAKE all go together, and the wait
stops needing to drop and retake wined3d_mutex around itself.

TWO THINGS NOT TAKEN FROM 11.15

  - max_frame_latency keeps the 11.0 value, device->max_frame_latency - 1,
    "subtract 1 for the implicit OpenGL latency". 11.15 uses the device value
    unreduced, which lets the producer run one further frame ahead. That is a
    latency and throughput change that nobody here has measured, and shipping it
    inside a hang fix would confound both.
  - WINED3D_SWAPCHAIN_FRAME_LATENCY_WAITABLE_OBJECT does not exist in 11.0, so
    the wait upstream guards with that flag is simply unconditional here, and
    wined3d_swapchain_{get,set}_max_frame_latency and the waitable-object getter
    are not backported.

TOKEN CONSERVATION, WHICH IS THE ONLY WAY THIS FAILS

A token taken and not returned is a producer that never presents again -- the
same class of freeze, arrived at from the other side. So:

  - the release is the LAST statement of wined3d_cs_exec_present(), which has no
    early return; the comment there says so, because adding one would be the bug.
  - wined3d_swapchain_present() returns the token on its one early-out, the
    !back_buffers path. Upstream 11.15 consumes a token there. Repeated failures
    would walk the count to zero and block the next good present forever.
  - swapchain_set_max_frame_latency() hands out the difference when the limit is
    raised. Lowering it only binds tokens not yet issued -- nothing can recall one
    a presenting thread is holding -- which is also upstream's behaviour, and d3d8
    exposes no way to change this at all, so on this port it is set once.

ON THIS FORK'S PRESENTER

The token comes back when the CS thread has finished executing that PRESENT,
which includes whatever the dmabuf presenter does synchronously inside
swapchain_ops->swapchain_present(). That is exactly where InterlockedDecrement()
sat before, so the coupling to the compositor is unchanged by this patch. The
semaphore bounds how far the producer runs ahead of the WineD3D CS worker; it is
not a compositor fence and must not be turned into one.

THE WITNESS

mgs2_present_watch goes to version 2 and changes in kind. The old fields said
"the wait timed out", which cannot separate a lost wake from a stalled consumer --
from the producer they look identical. The new ones report the gap between the two
halves: acquires climbing while releases stands still, parked at
max_frame_latency, is the CS thread wedged inside a handler, which is what was
actually captured on 2026-08-23. blocking_waits and longest_wait_ms come from a
zero-timeout attempt before the blocking one, which costs the same single wait
call in the common case where a token is free.

There is no reader for this struct yet; it is published for the freeze capture to
pick up when one is next taken.

